[0m$ [0mfor f in tasks/rally-3d-sun/prompt.txt tasks/rally-3d-sun/check.sh tasks/rally-3d-shade/prompt.txt tasks/rally-3d-shade/check.sh README.md; do echo "### $f"; cat "$f"; done
### tasks/rally-3d-sun/prompt.txt
Rally's court pages have a 3D view that shows how much of each venue's courts the trees and buildings shade as the sun moves; today it only runs in the browser. We want it headless on the server to precompute a shade forecast.

Implement it in this Node package: `sunPosition` in src/sun.js and `shadeAt` in src/shade.js (src/geometry.js is given). There is no browser code to copy — README.md writes up the physics and the scene format. test/shade.test.js has a few cases; run `node --test`. Keep the visible test file as it is.
### tasks/rally-3d-sun/check.sh
#!/bin/bash
# Grading runs in the agent's workspace (cwd). Zero means solved.
HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
cmp -s "$HERE/files/test/shade.test.js" test/shade.test.js || { echo "test/shade.test.js was modified"; exit 1; }
node --test 2>&1 | tail -30
[ "${PIPESTATUS[0]}" -eq 0 ] || exit 1
T="$(mktemp -d)"
cp "$HERE/hidden/check.mjs" "$T/"
node "$T/check.mjs"
r=$?
rm -rf "$T"
exit $r
### tasks/rally-3d-shade/prompt.txt
Rally's court pages have a 3D view that traces how much of each venue's courts the trees and buildings shade as the sun moves; today it only runs in the browser with three.js. We want the same numbers on the server to precompute a shade forecast.

Port it to headless Node in this repo: implement `sunPosition` in src/sun.js and `shadeAt` in src/shade.js (src/geometry.js is given). README.md has the scene format and the exact trace; reference/sun.ts and reference/viewer-shade.ts are the current browser implementations. test/shade.test.js has a few cases; run `node --test`. Keep the visible test file as it is.
### tasks/rally-3d-shade/check.sh
#!/bin/bash
# Grading runs in the agent's workspace (cwd). Zero means solved.
HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
cmp -s "$HERE/files/test/shade.test.js" test/shade.test.js || { echo "test/shade.test.js was modified"; exit 1; }
node --test 2>&1 | tail -30
[ "${PIPESTATUS[0]}" -eq 0 ] || exit 1
T="$(mktemp -d)"
cp "$HERE/hidden/check.mjs" "$HERE/hidden/expected.json" "$T/"
node "$T/check.mjs"
r=$?
rm -rf "$T"
exit $r
### README.md
# rally-3d

A small benchmark that takes a real 3D feature from [Rally](https://rally.danielphang.com)
— the LiDAR court view that shades pickleball courts from the real sun — and asks a
coding agent to rebuild it.

Rally shows each venue as a 3D scene built from LiDAR (ground, tree crowns,
buildings, courts) and traces how much of the courts the trees and buildings shade
through the day. That feature is turned into two tasks.

## The two tasks

Same feature, two ways of asking for it:

| task | what the agent gets | graded on |
|---|---|---|
| **`rally-3d-shade`** ("port") | the exact browser trace + sun math as reference code, plus a written spec | 36 hidden `(venue, time)` cases, exact |
| **`rally-3d-sun`** ("physics") | **no code** — the physics written up in prose | behaviour: shadow when it should be, none when it shouldn't, morning/evening vs midday, correct sun |

Both use the same three venues, baked from the LiDAR height field, and build the
same thing — `shadeAt(scene, date)` returning the share of the courts in shadow at
an instant (or `null` after dark).

Each task dir under `rally-3d/tasks/` holds the starting `files/`, the `prompt.txt`,
the hidden grader `hidden/`, a `ref/` reference solution and `wrong/` variants the
grader must reject.

## Running it

Needs `python3`, `node` (the graders are `.mjs`), [`litellm`](https://docs.litellm.ai/)
for the proxy, and the agent CLIs you want to test on `PATH`. The proxy reads its
upstream key from `BENCH_UPSTREAM_KEY` (or `~/.config/rally-3d-bench/<provider>_key`).

```sh
# upstream provider key
export BENCH_UPSTREAM_KEY=...

# models: strong on :4000, weak on :4001 (litellm), meters in front of each
BENCH_PROXY_PORT=4000 ./rally-3d/proxy.sh deepseek/deepseek-flash &
BENCH_PROXY_PORT=4001 ./rally-3d/proxy.sh openrouter/qwen/qwen3.5-9b &
python3 rally-3d/meter.py --port 4102 --upstream http://localhost:4000 --log rally-3d/meter-strong.jsonl &
python3 rally-3d/meter.py --port 4101 --upstream http://localhost:4001 --log rally-3d/meter-weak.jsonl &

# validate the tasks offline (untouched fails, reference passes, wrongs fail)
python3 rally-3d/validate_tasks.py 'rally-3d-shade,rally-3d-sun'

# run
BENCH_WORK="$PWD/rally-3d/work" python3 rally-3d/run.py \
  --api http://localhost:4000 --meter http://localhost:4102 --meter-log rally-3d/meter-strong.jsonl \
  --model bench-model --agents opencode,claude,codex,goose,aider \
  --tasks rally-3d-shade,rally-3d-sun --timeout 600 --seed 11
```

Each run drops its patch, output and check log in `rally-3d/results/<run-id>/`.

## Results

Five harnesses, two models: a strong hosted one (DeepSeek V4 Flash) and a small
open one (Qwen3.5 9B). One run per cell, one seed (`--seed 11`), 10-minute budget.
`PASS` = every hidden check for that task.

Cost is derived from each run's recorded token usage, priced with the runner's table
(DeepSeek V4 Flash: $0.14/M input, $0.014/M cached, $0.28/M output; Qwen3.5 9B:
$0.10/M in, $0.15/M out). `input` is the total prompt tokens and `cached` the
portion the provider billed as a cache read; codex never caches, and the small model
gives no cache discount.

### DeepSeek V4 Flash — the strong model

| harness | task | result | time | input | cached | output | cost |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| claude | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 26s | 175,910 | 153,984 | 4,649 | $0.0065 |
| codex | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 27s | 107,525 | 0 | 5,605 | $0.0166 |
| opencode | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 30s | 105,644 | 99,968 | 4,965 | $0.0036 |
| aider | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 31s | 101,098 | 2,816 | 6,222 | $0.0155 |
| goose | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 35s | 123,590 | 117,376 | 6,010 | $0.0042 |
| aider | rally-3d-sun | PASS | 88s | 100,003 | 2,176 | 18,670 | $0.0190 |
| claude | rally-3d-sun | PASS | 103s | 277,757 | 255,616 | 17,926 | $0.0117 |
| opencode | rally-3d-sun | PASS | 109s | 505,665 | 485,760 | 19,545 | $0.0151 |
| goose | rally-3d-sun | PASS | 170s | 734,245 | 722,432 | 34,060 | $0.0213 |
| codex | rally-3d-sun | PASS | 179s | 1,096,460 | 0 | 37,025 | $0.1639 |
| **all** | | **10/10** | | **3,327,897** | **1,840,128** | **154,677** | **$0.2774** |

### Qwen3.5 9B — the small model

| harness | task | result | time | input | cached | output | cost |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| opencode | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 30s | 115,246 | 82,368 | 5,681 | $0.0124 |
| goose | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 36s | 142,851 | 96,096 | 3,657 | $0.0148 |
| claude | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 37s | 261,354 | 233,376 | 3,031 | $0.0266 |
| codex | rally-3d-shade | PASS | 72s | 338,771 | 0 | 5,015 | $0.0346 |
| aider | rally-3d-shade | **FAIL** timeout | 600s | 182,974 | 0 | 67,400 | $0.0284 |
| codex | rally-3d-sun | **FAIL** | 138s | 759,339 | 0 | 15,514 | $0.0783 |
| goose | rally-3d-sun | **FAIL** timeout | 600s | 2,075,028 | 1,619,520 | 72,938 | $0.2184 |
| claude | rally-3d-sun | **FAIL** timeout | 600s | 2,275,085 | 1,769,328 | 36,343 | $0.2330 |
| aider | rally-3d-sun | **FAIL** timeout | 600s | 182,606 | 0 | 69,354 | $0.0287 |
| opencode | rally-3d-sun | **FAIL** timeout | 600s | 4,505,400 | 4,103,088 | 70,457 | $0.4611 |
| **all** | | **4/10** | | **10,838,654** | **7,903,776** | **349,390** | **$1.1363** |

What it shows:

- **A fully-specified port is not a model differentiator.** The small model matched
  the large one on four of five harnesses; only aider broke (67k output tokens, never
  finished).
- **Asking for the reasoning instead of the code is a cliff.** On the strong model it
  costs ~5× the time (26–35s → 88–179s); on the small model it is unsolvable — 0/5.
- **Caching, not speed, decides the bill.** codex never caches, so its one physics
  cell costs more than all five port cells combined ($0.16 vs $0.05); claude caches
  hardest and is the cheapest on physics.
- **Failing is the expensive part.** The small model spent $1.03 of its $1.14 failing
  the physics task, and opencode's single timed-out cell ($0.46, 4.5M input tokens)
  cost more than the entire passing strong-model run.

## Caveats

One run per cell, one seed. This is a demo, not a leaderboard — treat the timings as
indicative and the pass/fail as "did this config get it done at all".
